# Changelog

Ticket-by-ticket record of changes from live beta testing. Newest first.

---

## TKT-00009 — Notification-badge queries returning 503

**2026-08-13** · Priority: Medium · Reported by John Tucker (filed by Claude Cowork) · Branch `beta-0.0.1-tkt0009`

### Symptom

Two Supabase REST calls failed with HTTP 503 on every single page load,
site-wide: the unread-messages count and the pending-bookings count that
power the header's notification badges. Every other query (profiles,
favorites, reviews) succeeded. No error reached the user — the badges just
never showed a number, even with a real pending booking sitting there.

### Root cause

Confirmed reproducible from the code. `message.repository.ts` and
`booking.repository.ts` were the only two places in the app using
`.select('id', { count: 'exact', head: true })`. An exact count can't stop
at a `LIMIT`, so Postgres has to evaluate RLS for every candidate row to
produce it. The unread-message count made this worse than it needed to be:
its own filter (`is_read=false`, `sender_id<>me`) never scoped by user at
all — that was left entirely to messages' SELECT policy, a correlated
`EXISTS` subquery against `bookings` evaluated once per candidate row, the
single most expensive query shape anywhere in the schema. Both badge
components remount on every client-side navigation (rendered per-page
rather than once in a shared layout), so this was firing constantly across
the whole site and periodically saturating the connection pool as a 503 —
which also explains the report's secondary note of occasional 503s on the
app's own page data requests, since they share the same pool.

Both call sites also swallowed their errors completely: neither notifier
read `isError`/`error`, so a 503 just rendered as "0" with nothing in the
console to explain it.

### Changes

**`supabase/migrations/0020_notification_counts.sql`** (new) — two
`SECURITY DEFINER` functions, `get_unread_message_count()` and
`get_pending_booking_count()`, that do the same user-scoping as an explicit
`JOIN`/`WHERE` instead of a per-row RLS check, reading `auth.uid()`
internally rather than taking it as an argument (a `SECURITY DEFINER`
function bypasses RLS entirely, so a caller-supplied id would let any
authenticated user read anyone else's counts). `EXECUTE` is revoked from
`anon`/`PUBLIC` and granted only to `authenticated`, matching the grant
discipline in 0015.

**`packages/supabase/src/repositories/message.repository.ts`** —
`getUnreadCount()` now calls the RPC instead of the `count: 'exact'` query;
dropped the now-unnecessary `userId` argument.

**`packages/supabase/src/repositories/booking.repository.ts`** —
`getPendingRequestCount()` likewise now calls the RPC; dropped `ownerId`.

**`packages/supabase/src/hooks/useMessages.ts`** /
**`useBookings.ts`** — the two hook call sites updated to match (the `userId`
they already receive still drives the query key and `enabled` gate; it just
no longer gets threaded into the repo call).

**`apps/web/src/components/MessagesNotifier.tsx`** /
**`BookingsNotifier.tsx`** — both now `console.error('[toolshare] ...', err)`
on a failed count, matching the existing non-fatal-error convention
elsewhere in the app (`loadTools.ts`, `ToolPhotoPicker.tsx`). The badge
itself still just omits on failure rather than showing an error state — it's
a background poll, not worth interrupting the header for — but a future
503 will now show up in the console instead of reading as "no unread
messages."

### Deferred — not done in this pass

The research behind this ticket also flagged that `SiteHeader` (and the
notifier components it renders via `HeaderAccount`) is mounted individually
in ~19 page files instead of once in the root layout, which is *why* these
queries were firing on every navigation rather than just periodically.
`SiteHeader` takes a per-page `showSearch` prop (search bar shows on
`/tools/[id]`, `/search`, a few others; not on most pages), so hoisting it
into `app/layout.tsx` isn't a drop-in change — it needs a route-aware way to
decide `showSearch` from the layout, and touches every one of those 19
pages. That's a real reduction in query frequency worth doing, but it's a
separate, larger structural change from the query-shape fix above, and out
of scope for this pass. Filing as a follow-up rather than doing it under
this ticket.

### Verification

- `pnpm --filter @toolshare/supabase typecheck` — clean.
- `pnpm --filter @toolshare/web typecheck` — clean.
- **Not verified against a live database in this session** — Docker wasn't
  running, so the local Supabase stack couldn't come up to actually apply
  migration `0020` and exercise the new RPCs. Before merging: `supabase db
  reset` locally, then confirm both `/rest/v1/rpc/get_unread_message_count`
  and `/rest/v1/rpc/get_pending_booking_count` return a correct count for a
  signed-in test user (not just 200), and that an unread message / pending
  booking still shows up in the badge.

---

## TKT-00007 — Booking detail photo shows a blank placeholder

**2026-08-13** · Priority: Low · Reported by John Tucker (filed by Claude Cowork) · Branch `beta-0.0.1-tkt0007`

### Symptom

Booked a test rental end-to-end (Makita 18V LXT Cordless Drill), then opened
the resulting booking detail page. The tool photo shown correctly on the
listing/search page rendered as a blank gray placeholder on
`/bookings/[id]`.

### Investigation

Checked the render path against every other place the app shows a booking's
tool photo. It's identical: `BookingDetail.tsx` reads
`booking.tool?.photo_urls?.[0]` and falls back to the same placeholder icon
as `BookingRow.tsx` (the `/bookings` list row) and `ToolCard.tsx` (search
grid) — same field, same `tool:tools(...)` join in `BOOKING_SELECT`, same
open `USING (true)` RLS policy on `tools`, same full public-URL values in
storage. No trigger clears `photo_urls` on booking creation.

The placeholder only renders when that value is empty at render time — this
was the intended empty state firing, not an image request failing — which
rules out a query, RLS, or Next/Image config bug and points at the data
being genuinely absent for that one render.

### Root cause

**Not reproducible from static code review.** No defect found in the render
path itself. The most plausible explanation is a cold-cache race specific to
the "Confirm → View booking" flow: `useCreateBooking`'s `onSuccess` handler
invalidated the renter/owner list queries but never populated the
booking-detail query, so clicking "View booking" straight off the
confirmation screen was the one moment guaranteed to hit a brand-new,
unwarmed fetch instead of already-known data. Nothing proves this was the
actual cause, but it's the one code path that differs between this report
and any later, ordinary visit to the same page.

### Changes

**`packages/supabase/src/hooks/useBookings.ts`** — `useCreateBooking`'s
`onSuccess` now seeds `bookingKeys.detail(id)` with the full booking object
the insert already returns (it's selected through `BOOKING_SELECT`, so it
already carries `tool.photo_urls`). "View booking" now renders from that
seeded data immediately instead of racing a fresh fetch.

### Verification

- `pnpm --filter @toolshare/supabase typecheck` — clean.
- This was a defensive fix for a report that couldn't be reproduced from
  source, so there's no failing repro to turn green here. Next live test:
  confirm the photo appears immediately after "View booking" on a fresh
  booking; if it still goes blank, escalate as a data issue and check
  whether `tool.photo_urls` is actually empty in the Supabase response for
  that specific booking.

