## TKT-00002 — Easy pass signup (Google)

**2026-08-08** · Priority: Medium · Suggested by Adrian Teply · Branch `beta-0.0.1-tkt-00002`

### What was actually in the way

Not the OAuth wiring. `/api/auth/callback` was already provider-agnostic — it
exchanges any `?code=` for a session and honours `?next=` — so Google needed no
new endpoint at all.

The obstacle was the **pilot gate**. Production gates `auth.users` inserts with
`enforce_invite_code()`, which reads `raw_user_meta_data->>'invite_code'`. A
Google signup carries Google's claims (`email`, `name`, `picture`, `sub`) and no
invite code, and `signInWithOAuth` has no `data:` option to smuggle one through
the way `signUp` does. A naive implementation would therefore have **worked
locally and failed in production** with a raw "Database error saving new user",
because migration 0016 deliberately omits that trigger from the local schema.

### Changes

**`supabase/migrations/0019_google_signup_allowlist.sql`** (new)

- `approved_emails` table — allowlist fed from the waiting list. RLS on with no
  policies, same shape as `invite_codes`, reachable only through
  `SECURITY DEFINER` functions. A `CHECK (email = lower(email))` keeps the key
  from holding two casings of one address.
- `enforce_invite_code()` replaced: a signup is accepted when its address is on
  the allowlist, otherwise the existing invite-code path runs unchanged. The
  trigger itself is still **not** created locally, for the reason 0016
  documents — attaching it would reject the demo rows `seed.sql` inserts.
- `check_invite_code()` added to the local schema. It was production-only,
  which made the registration form's pre-check throw instead of returning
  false, and meant `e2e/invite-signup.spec.ts` **could never pass locally**.
  It is read-only and gates nothing, so unlike the trigger there was no reason
  to keep it out.
- `handle_new_user()` extended: `display_name` now falls back through
  `full_name` → `name` → email local-part, and `avatar_url` picks up Google's
  `picture`. Without this every Google user would land as "jane.doe" with no
  avatar.

**App**

- `GoogleButton.tsx` (new) — `signInWithOAuth` pointed at the existing PKCE
  callback, preserving `next`. Inline SVG mark so the strict CSP needs no
  remote asset. Rendered on both `/login` and `/register`, above the email
  form, with the invite-code path still intact beneath it.
- `api/auth/callback/route.ts` — distinguishes *why* an exchange failed. A
  signup the gate rejected now redirects to `?error=not_approved`, and a
  dismissed consent screen to `?error=cancelled`, instead of both reading as
  "Authentication failed".
- `login/page.tsx` — renders those two states: non-approved testers are pointed
  at the waiting list rather than told the site is broken.
- `supabase/config.toml` — `[auth.external.google]` block (disabled by
  default), plus `http://localhost:3000` added to `additional_redirect_urls`.
  Both entries were `127.0.0.1` only, while `next dev` and Playwright serve on
  `localhost`, and OAuth redirect matching is exact.
- `.env.example` / `CLAUDE.md` — the two Google env vars, the authorised
  redirect URI to register with Google, and how to allowlist a tester.

### Verification

The gate is the part that can't be checked by running the app locally, since
the trigger isn't attached there. Verified instead by attaching it inside a
transaction and exercising every path:

| Scenario | Result |
|---|---|
| Google, allowlisted, no invite code | allowed |
| Google, **not** allowlisted, no code | rejected — "An invite code is required" |
| Allowlist match is case-insensitive | allowed |
| Email+password with a valid code | allowed |
| Email+password with a bad code | rejected — "Invalid or expired invite code" |

The Google case also produced a profile named **"Jane Doe"** with an avatar,
confirming the `handle_new_user()` change.

- `pnpm typecheck` — clean across all 7 packages.
- `apps/web` unit — 51/53. New: 4 `GoogleButton` tests (provider, callback URL,
  preserved `next`, error surfacing) and 7 callback-route tests covering each
  redirect branch. The route test needs `// @vitest-environment node`; the
  shared config is jsdom.
- `packages/domain` — 21/21.
- **Full end-to-end suite against a production build — 21/21, green for the
  first time this session.** `e2e/invite-signup.spec.ts` had been failing since
  before this batch of tickets and now passes.

### Scope note

The OAuth handshake itself is not automated — it leaves the app for Google's
consent screen and needs real credentials and a real account. What the button
hands Supabase, and how the callback branches, are covered by unit tests; the
end-to-end spec covers the entry points rendering and the rejection messaging.

### Regression this caused, and the fix

Labelling the button "Sign in with Google" broke the `signIn` helper in four
existing specs, whose `getByRole('button', { name: /sign in/i })` then matched
two buttons. The label is now "Continue with Google" (also the conventional
wording), and every spec's selector was tightened to
`{ name: 'Sign in', exact: true }` so another button mentioning "sign in"
cannot break them again.

### Before this ships

1. Create the OAuth client in Google Cloud; authorised redirect URI is
   `https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback`.
2. Enable Google in the hosted Supabase dashboard — production does not read
   `config.toml`.
3. Add the production site URL to the redirect allow-list.
4. Populate `approved_emails`. It is currently seeded with one address
   (`tucker@bitcrafttech.com`); every other tester needs adding, or Google
   sign-in will reject them.

