## TKT-00005 — Mobile navigation only showed account and messages

**2026-08-08** · Priority: Medium · Reported by Nathaniel Tucker · Branch `bet-0.0.1-tkt-00005`

### Symptom

"Navigation Bar in mobile only shows the account and message links in vertical
positions." Bookings, adding a tool, messages and the bug report were all
expected to be reachable.

### Root cause

Straightforward, and exactly as reported. The header hid its three nav links
below `md` (`hidden md:flex`) and Favorites / Bug report / Bookings below `sm`
(`hidden sm:flex`). Below 640px that left a signed-in user with precisely two
things: the Messages icon and their avatar.

There was **no hamburger, drawer, sheet or bottom bar anywhere in the web app** —
no `Menu` icon import, no mobile nav component. The `md:`-hidden links were
simply unreachable on a phone. The only escape hatch was the footer, which
carries "Lend a tool" but no Bookings, Messages or Bug report.

The design system had already anticipated this: `packages/ui`'s `TabBar`
documents itself as the mobile counterpart to `Navbar`, complete with
safe-area insets and badge support. `SiteHeader` just never used it.

### Changes

A single breakpoint (`md`) now governs the whole navigation, so every
destination is reachable from exactly one place at any width.

- **`apps/web/src/components/MobileTabBar.tsx`** (new) — bottom tab bar,
  `md:hidden`. Signed in: Home · Browse · Lend · Bookings · Messages, with the
  same live pending-request and unread-message badges the desktop header shows,
  driven by the same realtime subscriptions. Signed out: Home · Browse · How it
  works · Sign in. Mirrors the Expo app's tab bar so both platforms navigate
  alike.
- **`packages/ui/src/navigation/tab-bar.tsx`** — `TabBarItem` gained an `as`
  prop so it can render `next/link` and keep navigation client-side.
  `legacyBehavior` is gone in Next 16, so this was the clean route.
- **`HeaderAccount.tsx`** — Favorites, Bookings and Messages moved from `sm:`
  to `md:`, matching the tab bar's `md:hidden`. At `sm` the two would otherwise
  have overlapped and rendered every destination twice. Bug report lost its
  responsive gate entirely: it has no tab, and phone testers are exactly who
  needs it.
- **`app/layout.tsx`** — renders the tab bar and pads the body by
  `calc(4rem + env(safe-area-inset-bottom))` below `md` so the fixed bar cannot
  cover the footer.

`ToolBookingPanel`'s sticky CTA is `sticky top-24`, not bottom-fixed, so there
is no collision with the new bar.

### Bug found by the new test — `packages/ui/src/components/toast.tsx`

The toast viewport is `fixed inset-x-0 bottom-0 z-[60]` and had no
`pointer-events-none`. Empty or not, it spanned the full width at the bottom of
the screen above everything else, so it **swallowed every click aimed at the
tab bar** — the bar rendered correctly and was completely untappable on a
phone. Caught by the "tapping a tab actually navigates" end-to-end test, which
timed out with the toast container reported as intercepting pointer events.

The container is now `pointer-events-none` and individual toasts opt back in
with `pointer-events-auto`. This also affected anything else that might sit at
the bottom of the viewport, so it was a latent bug, not one this ticket
introduced.

### Verification

- `pnpm typecheck` — clean across all 7 packages.
- `apps/web` unit — 40/42. New: 4 `MobileTabBar` tests (destinations per auth
  state, badges, active-section matching including nested routes) and 2
  `HeaderAccount` tests. The pre-existing `HeaderAccount` tests asserted link
  *ordering* only, which is precisely why a responsive-visibility regression
  was invisible to the suite; the new ones assert the breakpoint classes.
- `packages/domain` — 21/21.
- `e2e/tkt-00005-mobile-nav.spec.ts` (new) — 5 tests at a 390×844 viewport:
  every requested destination reachable, tabs actually navigate, signed-out
  variant, the bar does not cover the footer, and at desktop width the bar
  gives way to the header so nothing is offered twice. Viewport is set with
  `test.use` per describe-block rather than by adding a Playwright project, so
  the rest of the suite still runs once at desktop size.
- Full end-to-end suite against a production build — 16/17 (the one failure is
  the pre-existing `invite-signup` spec).

### Still desktop-only

Favorites (`/favorites`) has no tab and remains `md:`-gated. That was already
true before this ticket and is outside what was reported; worth a decision
later on whether it belongs in the tab bar or on the profile page.

